WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:15.070
How much psychology can you put into a manga?

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:20.700
If I were to ask this question to Eiichiro
Oda, the author of One Piece, his answer would

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.440
probably be: Everything.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.860
Or at least something along that line.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:31.180
In fact, I would argue that the psychology
behind Oda´s characters is the single biggest

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:36.410
reason that his story appeals to such a broad
audience up to this day.

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:41.570
And I mean that quite literally; Regardless
of where you´re from or how old you are.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:47.770
You can enjoy the story as a teenager, looking
at it through innocent and excited eyes.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:53.160
Enjoying the fights, cheering for the characters,
and feeling the impact of the major emotional

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.620
moments of the show.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:59.520
However, a lot of the major characters in
the story also have their subtleties that

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:05.960
aren´t immediately noticeable without a certain
amount of personal life experience.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:11.150
Such nuances have been constructed in such
a way that they add a subtle, but impactful

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:17.170
portion of realism and depth that make them
more and more relatable throughout the years.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:21.700
These characters especially reflect parts
of the human mind and persona that might be

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:27.130
picked up upon only by someone who has had
a similar experience, or have had enough life

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:33.270
experience to consciously, or even unconsciously,
pick up on these subtle underlying themes

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.920
of some of these characters.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:41.490
Psychology is intertwined all throughout One
Piece and can be found distinctly for each

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:42.490
character.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:48.190
Today, however, I want to explore with you
the thematic mind of one person in particular;

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:53.710
to examine what impact his theme has on the
audience and to demonstrate how psychology

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:58.300
can cleverly be made visible in a medium like
Manga or Anime.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:01.119
Let´s talk about the purple tiger.

00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:07.200
Let´s talk about Marine Admiral Fujitora;
his blind justice and why it makes him the

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.920
best developed admiral out of the lot.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:18.230
Fujitora, or as he is truly called, Isshō,
is a broadly built, middle-aged man that has

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:21.670
been recruited into the ranks of the marines
after the time-skip.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:27.810
His most prominent features are the deep scars
over his eyes that mark the spots where Isshō

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:32.480
blinded himself with his sword after having
witnessed something truly horrific.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:38.670
His character design is dominated by his samurai-like
appearance, that have made it easy to speculate

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:42.060
that he has some connection to Wano.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:47.540
Same as the other admirals, Fujitora´s character
has been based on a Japanese actor.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:55.460
In Fujitora´s case, this is Shintaro Katsu
or more specifically on his role as Zatoichi,

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:57.150
the blind swordsman.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:04.700
Both characters share a number of distinct
similarities: Both carry a shikomizue, a cane

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:08.349
sword that they use for both walking and fighting.

00:03:08.349 --> 00:03:13.070
Both share a love for gambling, a theme that
is especially pronounced with Isshō, who

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:17.690
not only likes to gamble literally but he
also likes to roll the dice when it comes

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:20.390
to fights and important decisions.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:25.910
He literally does so, when torn between duty
and moral, having to decide whether or not

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:28.840
to assist Doflamingo against the Straw Hats.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:34.360
We can also see this in his standoff with
Law and Doflamingo, where he brings down a

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:38.040
meteor without certainty who or what it would
hit.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.040
Also, both Isshō and Zatoichi share a strong
sense of compassion, putting the interest

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.040
of the people before their own.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:44.190
They both try to help the people out of their
misery as best as they can.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:50.650
In that sense, both of them are also extremely
humble, not taking a lot of pride in themselves.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:55.349
Especially compared to some of his colleagues,
Fujitora certainly does not see himself as

00:03:55.349 --> 00:03:59.080
superior or as a force of authority to others.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:04.319
Notably, while he has enjoyed more screen
time than most of the other admirals in the

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:08.230
story, there is not much we know about Isshō´s
past.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:14.170
We DO know that he was born somewhere on the
Grand Line - possibly Wano - and that at some

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:18.940
point in his life, he decided to blind himself
after being overwhelmed by the cruelty of

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:19.940
the world.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:25.600
A possibility in this regard is that this
event had something to do with Wano and the

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.210
horrific execution of Oden by Orochi and Kaido.

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:33.830
Should he actually be from Wano, there would
certainly have been enough reasons to explain

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:35.390
his painful past.

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:40.620
However, whatever the trigger for this tragic
decision might have been, we can be sure that

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.660
Fujitora has suffered through a lot in his
life.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:48.850
It is a pain literally made visible to the
audience through the scars on his face.

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:54.540
They serve as a constant reminder of his suffering
and hardship and the trauma it must have left

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.150
in his heart.

00:04:56.150 --> 00:05:00.531
Despite being a Marine Admiral and, in that
sense, an antagonist to Luffy and the straw

00:05:00.531 --> 00:05:06.870
hats, it becomes clear very fast that Isshō
is not meant to be an enemy to the crew.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:12.250
Right from his introduction, Oda makes it
clear that here we are dealing with a very

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:17.830
kind and emotional man, who does not realize
he is being cheated on by Doflamingo´s men

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:19.229
during their gambling.

00:05:19.229 --> 00:05:24.810
His kind heart and the respect he shows towards
our protagonists makes it very easy for us

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.710
as the audience to empathize with him.

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:33.660
When he stops Zoro from attacking Doflamingo,
he literally apologizes for his behavior after

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:37.840
having been helped by the crew earlier in
the casino.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:43.660
In general, every major confrontation Fujitora
has over the course of the Dressrosa arc involves

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:47.870
some sort of moral and emotional dialog with
his opponents.

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:52.820
No matter if it´s sharing his concerns over
Dressrosa with Sabo, stopping Doflamingo from

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:56.530
killing Law, or even his short clash with
Luffy.

00:05:56.530 --> 00:06:00.680
At no point do we perceive him as a threat.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:06.229
Much rather, we see him as someone with good
intentions who is opposing Luffy and Co. based

00:06:06.229 --> 00:06:09.240
on his responsibility as a marine.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:14.600
This however does not mean that Isshō is
not intimidating.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:20.960
Just the opposite, Oda makes it painfully
clear just how powerful the Admiral is.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:26.910
His skills as a swordsman as well as his powerful
gravity devil fruit make it very obvious why

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:31.902
Fujitora holds the rank of a Marine Admiral
despite having been drafted into the Marines

00:06:31.902 --> 00:06:34.710
merely two years ago.

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:40.260
But as we all know, power alone only seldomly
makes for a fascinating character.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:46.880
And Fujitora in particular is a wonderful
example of how real human psychology can be

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:52.979
incorporated into a fictional character, creating
a distinct and relatable emotional response

00:06:52.979 --> 00:06:57.919
in an audience.

00:06:57.919 --> 00:07:02.600
In order to be able to dissect and understand
the true power of Isshō’s personality from

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:07.330
a storytelling perspective, we have to start
with his wound.

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:12.960
However, counterintuitively to the general
perception of Shounen manga, we are not talking

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:17.970
about the scars on his face or any other physical
wound Fujitora has suffered over the course

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:19.729
of his life.

00:07:19.729 --> 00:07:25.389
Much rather, these scars serve as a visual
representation of a much deeper, psychological

00:07:25.389 --> 00:07:27.300
wound he has been dealt.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:32.569
This wound has its origin in unseen, horrible
events the samurai had to suffer through in

00:07:32.569 --> 00:07:34.430
the past.

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:40.150
No matter what it is that he had to witness,
it must have been so terrible that Isshō

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:44.800
felt no other way to deal with his pain other
than to mutilate himself to escape from the

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:46.640
cruelty of the world.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:52.940
Grounded in this emotional wound is the fundamental
weakness, that Oda has chosen for his intricate

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:53.970
character.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:59.660
The weakness of a character can be defined
as a lie about the world, a perceived false

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:05.380
truth, if you will, that they wrongfully believe
in and thus have to learn to overcome.

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:10.539
For Fujitora, this weakness seems to be the
belief that piracy is an evil; that has to

00:08:10.539 --> 00:08:14.270
be destroyed in order to bring lasting peace
to the world.

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:19.979
This suggests to us, that his past experiences
most likely involved the deeds of pirates

00:08:19.979 --> 00:08:22.410
in one way or another.

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:27.729
This weakness subsequently reveals to us the
psychological need of the character that determines

00:08:27.729 --> 00:08:30.069
his character development.

00:08:30.069 --> 00:08:34.990
In other words, the need is the solution to
the characters weakness that he has to acquire

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:37.289
through his action.

00:08:37.289 --> 00:08:43.079
In Fujitora´s case, this clearly is the need
to create a more nuanced world view and letting

00:08:43.079 --> 00:08:45.740
go of his hatred for pirates in general.

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:50.730
However, to turn the journey towards this
answer into a threat of story that yields

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:57.290
true moral insights and experiences for both
the character and the reader alike, the psychological

00:08:57.290 --> 00:09:00.480
need is usually unknown to the character itself.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:06.089
Thus, at the beginning of the Dressrosa arc,
Isshō appears to have a very clear imagine

00:09:06.089 --> 00:09:09.360
of what is good and what is bad in this world.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:14.720
To mask his emotional wound and avoid dealing
with it, he hides it behind a mask.

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:21.130
We can see this in all of the fights and confrontations
that he has, where he lets go of his kind

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:26.279
and gentle nature and takes on a hard and
determined stance.

00:09:26.279 --> 00:09:32.110
As a result, we, the audience, struggle with
him as he struggles between the ideals of

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:37.990
the world government, the revolutionaries,
Doflamingo and the Straw hats.

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:42.649
His desire after all, is to save and protect
the people of Dressrosa.

00:09:42.649 --> 00:09:49.290
In that sense, the entire Dressrosa arc serves
as an overarching representation of what determines

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.070
good and evil.

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:56.800
And Fujitora, who has to figure out who stands
for what on this island, serves as a sort

00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:58.879
of personification of this struggle.

00:09:58.879 --> 00:10:04.480
At the same time, this makes the Dressrosa
story arc his central character development

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:06.100
arc.

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:11.189
To put it into the broader context of the
other admirals, the change he undergoes is

00:10:11.189 --> 00:10:16.480
basically from Akainu to Aokiji, roughly speaking.

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:21.819
To explain this analogy, let us take one step
back.

00:10:21.819 --> 00:10:30.199
The first step in Isshō’s character development
happens before we ever get to meet him.

00:10:30.199 --> 00:10:35.529
Joining the Marines to fight the evil that
has caused him so much pain already is a major

00:10:35.529 --> 00:10:37.449
step for him.

00:10:37.449 --> 00:10:43.350
It suggests that, despite literally blinding
himself to not see any more of it, he at some

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:47.019
point has decided to face evil again.

00:10:47.019 --> 00:10:51.300
How he reached that resolve and determination,
we do not know yet.

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:56.930
However, it also shows us that Fujitora believed
the Marines to be a force of good, standing

00:10:56.930 --> 00:10:58.689
off against the pirates.

00:10:58.689 --> 00:11:05.350
Thus, Fujitora´s worldview at the beginning
of the arc comes very close to what we know

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:06.850
of Akainu – ideologically, at least.

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:11.839
However, this black and white view is very
quickly challenged by the actions taken by

00:11:11.839 --> 00:11:15.660
the straw hats and Doflamingo, who is affiliated
with the government.

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:22.079
As the events progress, we can see Isshō
struggle more and more with his belief until

00:11:22.079 --> 00:11:24.470
he finally lets Luffy go.

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:30.939
He breaks through his thought mask, by admitting
to himself that Luffy is actually a kind person.

00:11:30.939 --> 00:11:35.370
When we reach the end of the arc, we have
reached an approach that comes much closer

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:41.790
to Aokiji´s where Fujitora acts based on
his morality rather than his duty.

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:46.319
He learns to judge people based on their actions,
rather than their flag.

00:11:46.319 --> 00:11:51.509
Thus, he allows Luffy to escape, while taking
it on himself to abolish the system of the

00:11:51.509 --> 00:12:02.369
warlords that has caused so much suffering
to the world.

00:12:02.369 --> 00:12:06.600
What then is Fujitora´s ideal of blind justice?

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:11.339
To unearth that question, we once again have
to return to the fundamental theme of the

00:12:11.339 --> 00:12:17.079
admirals: Is everything we call evil, actually
evil?

00:12:17.079 --> 00:12:20.940
Every one of the admirals has their own answer
to this question.

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:26.179
And for Fujitora, I would argue that his sense
of Justice is very closely connected to his

00:12:26.179 --> 00:12:29.139
character arc, that we have just discussed.

00:12:29.139 --> 00:12:34.109
The first thought, when thinking about blind
justice, at least for me, is the image of

00:12:34.109 --> 00:12:36.069
lady justice.

00:12:36.069 --> 00:12:40.720
Often depicted as wearing a blindfold, she
represents impartiality.

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:46.249
In other words, the ideal that justice should
be applied without regard to wealth, power,

00:12:46.249 --> 00:12:47.300
or other status.

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:52.579
This description hits really close to home
when we think about Isshō’s actions and

00:12:52.579 --> 00:12:53.629
ideals.

00:12:53.629 --> 00:12:59.709
This of course is further supported by the
fact that Fujitora is literally blind.

00:12:59.709 --> 00:13:04.540
He physically cannot discriminate against
people based on their appearance.

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:09.920
As we can witness over the course of the story,
Fujitora decides to serve justice according

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:12.739
to people's deeds and not their affiliation.

00:13:12.739 --> 00:13:19.300
Thus, he increasingly conflicts with the world
government´s ideal of absolute justice.

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:24.639
To give you a better idea of this approach
to justice, let´s look at two impactful moments

00:13:24.639 --> 00:13:26.610
for his character.

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:31.250
The first takes place after his standoff with
Doflamingo and Law.

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:36.829
After Law is taken out by the warlord, Fujitora
steps in to prevent Doflamingo from taking

00:13:36.829 --> 00:13:38.120
Law´s life.

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:43.879
The fair and moral thing to do is to capture
Law, not execute him.

00:13:43.879 --> 00:13:50.790
And his job is to serve the justice he deserves,
not any more or less.

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:54.449
He thus protects Law from unjust treatment
by Doflamingo.

00:13:54.449 --> 00:13:59.199
The second instance is his confrontation with
Zoro.

00:13:59.199 --> 00:14:04.129
Being aware of the kindness the straw hats
showed towards him, he apologizes for standing

00:14:04.129 --> 00:14:10.309
in his way and openly verbalizes his internal
struggle that is building up at this point.

00:14:10.309 --> 00:14:16.620
What this shows us is that Fujitora is willing
to take responsibility for his actions.

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:21.829
Even if it means going against Akainu and
the Gorosei for letting Luffy go and bowing

00:14:21.829 --> 00:14:25.980
to king Riku and thus exposing the world government’s
failure.

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:32.199
It is the just thing to do and Fujitora serves
it just as lady justice does, blindfolded;

00:14:32.199 --> 00:14:34.709
in Fujitora’s case, literally blinded by
his own past.

00:14:34.709 --> 00:14:38.399
Thus, Isshō’s character truly is psychology
made visible.

00:14:38.399 --> 00:14:43.809
His scars literally represent his ideal of
blind justice.

00:14:43.809 --> 00:14:47.639
If you enjoyed this video, consider leaving
me a like and some of your thoughts in the

00:14:47.639 --> 00:14:48.819
comments below.

00:14:48.819 --> 00:14:53.359
Also, if you aren´t subscribed to the channel
yet, I hope I could convince you that it might

00:14:53.359 --> 00:14:55.149
be worth doing so.

00:14:55.149 --> 00:14:56.149
Welcome on board.

00:14:56.149 --> 00:14:58.149
I hope to see you around next time.

00:14:58.149 --> 00:14:59.259
Stay curious.

00:14:59.259 --> 00:15:00.259
AND.

00:15:00.259 --> 00:15:01.259
Until then.

00:15:01.259 --> 00:15:02.099
Have a wonderful week.

